in 2013 and is headquartered in La Jolla, California.","ceo_bio":"Mr. D. McDonald Armstrong | Founder, CEO & Chairman of the Board | Compensation: $5.1M (FY2025)","culture_narrative":"Employs approximately 439 people. Revenue per employee of $2.0M ranks in the top quartile among Financial Services peers, with revenue growing 41%/yr across its filed history. Each employee generates $932K of free cash flow annually. Stock-based compensation of $48K per employee reflects an ownership culture. CEO compensation is 3\u00d7 revenue per employee (modest relative to workforce productivity). Governance score flags low compensation governance risk (2/10).","financial_summary":["Palomar Holdings, Inc. is a mid-cap financial services business with a $4.3B market cap on $876M of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at +39% over the past three years, the kind of sustained demand persistence that marks a compounder. Operating margins are expanding by +2.0pp year-over-year, which is one of the strongest signals that the moat is deepening.","Returns on capital run at 22% against a 9% cost of capital \u2014 a 13-point spread that is the clearest signature of an economic moat.","The business throws off $409M in free cash flow \u2014 roughly 47 cents on every revenue dollar, which is the owner-earnings figure that actually belongs to shareholders, and cash conversion sits at 2.08\u00d7 so those profits are backed by real cash.","On the balance sheet, the balance sheet carries net cash, which is the gold standard: the business never has to make desperate decisions in a bad year.","Shareholder yield is only 2.0%, a thin return of capital, and insiders hold 2.7% of the shares."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Low reinvestment-driven growth suggests the compounding engine has stalled","sev":"amber","cat":"growth"},{"text":"Trading near 52-week highs \u2014 the market has already recognised the story (confirmed by 1 other valuation measure)","sev":"amber","cat":"valuation","magnitude"